THINK = {
1: r"Elimination is just the school method of subtracting one equation from another, done in an organised order so that it never gets lost. Each step makes the system simpler while keeping the same solutions. Hold two pictures in your head: lines crossing at a point, and a recipe that mixes columns to hit a target. Switching between them is half of linear algebra.",
2: r"A matrix is not a table to be memorised but a machine: feed it a vector, it returns another. Multiplying two matrices means running one machine after another, which is why the order matters (putting on socks then shoes is not shoes then socks). When a product confuses you, ask what each machine does to a single vector.",
3: r"A subspace is a flat sheet through the origin that is closed under adding and scaling, the natural home of solutions. The four fundamental subspaces answer four questions about a matrix: what can it reach, what does it destroy, and the same for its transpose. Try to see them as the answers to ``which $\mathbf b$ are reachable?'' and ``which $\mathbf x$ vanish?''",
4: r"A linear map is a rule that keeps straight lines straight and the origin fixed. The matrix is just that rule written in a chosen coordinate system, and changing the basis changes the description but not the map. Separate in your mind the thing (the map) from its description (the matrix).",
5: r"Orthogonality is the best kind of independence: perpendicular directions do not interfere, so you can handle them one at a time. Projection answers ``what part of $\mathbf b$ lies along this direction?'' and Gram--Schmidt manufactures perpendicular directions from any set. Whenever a problem is messy, ask if a perpendicular basis would make it simple.",
6: r"A determinant is one number that answers a yes/no question: does this machine squash space flat? Its size tells you how much volume is scaled, its sign whether orientation flips. Do not memorise the cofactor formula; remember the geometry and compute by elimination.",
7: r"Most vectors get turned and stretched by a matrix, but a few special directions are only stretched. Those are the eigenvectors, and in their directions the machine becomes simple multiplication. If you can break a vector into eigenvector pieces, repeating the machine a hundred times is easy: each piece is just scaled a hundred times.",
8: r"When no exact solution exists, the sensible answer is the closest point you can reach, and ``closest'' means the error is perpendicular to everything you can reach. That geometric sentence \emph{is} the normal equation. Fitting a line to data is therefore a projection, not a mysterious recipe.",
9: r"Symmetric matrices behave beautifully: their eigenvectors are perpendicular, so the matrix is just independent stretching along perpendicular axes. That is why they appear whenever there is a notion of energy, curvature or spread. When you meet a symmetric matrix, think ``ellipse with perpendicular axes.''",
10: r"The SVD says every matrix, however rectangular, is a rotation, then a stretch along perpendicular axes, then another rotation. The stretch sizes (singular values) rank the directions by importance. Keeping the big ones and dropping the small is compression; PCA is exactly that applied to centred data.",
11: r"The derivative is the best straight-line approximation to a function near a point; the gradient and Jacobian are the same idea for many inputs. Whenever a function is complicated, zoom in until it looks linear, and then linear algebra takes over. The chain rule says that zooming composes: multiply the local linear maps.",
12: r"Probability is the maths of being uncertain without being confused. Name the possible outcomes, assign each a weight, and let the rules (conditional probability, independence, expectation) do the bookkeeping. Bayes' rule is simply the instruction to update what you believe when you see new evidence.",
13: r"Data are one random draw from what could have happened, so anything computed from data wobbles. Statistics measures that wobble and asks which method wobbles least, or points to the truth on average. Maximum likelihood asks, ``which setting of the world makes what I saw least surprising?''",
14: r"Optimisation is walking downhill with your eyes closed, feeling the slope with your feet: the gradient tells you which way is up, so you step the other way. Convexity is the promise that there is one valley, so any downhill walk finds the bottom. Constraints are fences, and Lagrange multipliers are the force with which the fence pushes back.",
15: r"Information is surprise: a rare event tells you more than a common one. Entropy is the average surprise, cross-entropy is the surprise you suffer when you believe the wrong distribution, and KL divergence is the extra cost of being wrong. Seeing the log in the loss function as ``measure surprise'' makes it feel natural rather than arbitrary.",
16: r"By now each idea has a simple job: linear algebra to represent, probability to model uncertainty, optimisation to learn. Building them into one small library shows that a learning algorithm is a loop around the same few moves: predict, measure the error, adjust. Writing it yourself removes the mystery.",
}
